WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
Executive functions are the mental processes

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.660
that enable us to plan, focus attention, remember

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.260
instructions, and juggle multiple tasks successfully.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.980
We can distinguish between three components:

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.240
1. Working memory,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.920
2. Inhibitory control and

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.460
3.Cognitive flexibility.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.760
All 3 are interlinked and influence each other.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.020
Psychologist Deborah Phillips from Georgetown University,

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.840
calls it the "air traffic control system" of our brain.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.400
Like an air traffic control system has to
manage lots of airplanes

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.340
departing and landing with perfect timing,

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.620
we have to manage a lot of information and distractions simultaneously.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.300
Without good executive functions, disaster strikes.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.400
Let's look at three different components:

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.240
The Working memory is responsible for processing information.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.540
If well developed, it allows us to manage

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.820
multiple chunks of information at the same time.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.260
Complex tasks can be solved and deep ideas understood.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.800
Without much of it, our intelligence is limited.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.200
Inhibitory control describes our capability
to concentrate,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.880
regulates our emotions

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.500
and controls our behaviour during stressful situations.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.400
It's an essential skill if we want to change
a childhood habit.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.780
Without it, we might have trouble to control our behavior

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.800
and can come across as "weird."

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.360
Cognitive flexibility is the ability to adapt new tasks

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.140
quickly and to change our perspective.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.560
If we have little of it, we can’t adapt,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.120
get stuck in old thoughts and have tunnel vision.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.320
We come across as stubborn and uncooperative.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.600
To demonstrate their roles,

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.700
let’s compare two imaginary teenagers: Ann and Jay.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.300
Ann is a working memory monster.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.800
She can keep lots of ideas in her mind at
the given time

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.280
and mentally buffers digits to calculate them
in her head.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.880
Even if she gets interrupted or feels a change of emotions,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.800
she realizes, but is still able to continue 
what she had started.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.220
Jay’s working memory is tiny.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.120
What Ann solves in her head,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.680
he has to write down.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.320
It's hard for him to read complex sentences

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.460
or follow a longer train of thought.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:28.840
He gets distracted easily and starts thinking
about something else.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.600
At school, Ann is an Inhibitory Control Genius.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.940
She is fully in charge of her emotions

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.280
and how they are expressed in her behavior.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.380
If a teacher loses his temper,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.620
she remains calm and friendly.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.080
It's easy for her to focus for long periods of time.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.440
As a result, her work is good and the feedback
on it is positive.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.280
Jay's emotions instantly express themselves
in his behavior,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.380
which he often fails to control.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.120
He can get distracted easily

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.440
and hence he has difficulties to finish what he started.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.540
His work remains mediocre.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:09.280
This can lead to negative feedback and
a lower self esteem.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.740
Ann’s cognitive flexibility is great.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.520
She can change her perspective

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.100
and find ways around roadblocks.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.820
At school she adjusts from one subject to
the other in the speed of light.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.180
Jay has problems to solve problems,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.560
because his thinking is inflexible.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.200
To change his perspective in response to
constructive criticism,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.480
takes a lot of his mental energy.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.280
Even simple tasks, like switching from talking
to listening is hard for him.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.560
Others can find this annoying.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.820
Executive function develops mainly during
the first 5 years of our lives.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.800
A caring, playful and nourishing childhood

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.140
is our best bet to increase them.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.920
Free play trains our inhibitory control

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.820
and games practice our working memory.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.360
Playing an instrument trains our brain to
process the notes,

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.080
and to coordinate the right and the left hand simultaneously.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.900
If others are listening, we learn to control
our emotions.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.660
Movement is great as well [P].

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.460
In football each situation is new,

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.660
ball possession requires quick decisions

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.100
and builds cognitive flexibility.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:23.120
One study showed that kids that walked barefoot
each day for just 16 minutes,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.000
improved their working memory.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.840
Because when they do, they need to keep many
things in mind.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.440
With a lot of practise as kids,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.540
we become real executives of our own mind.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.980
This then allows us to excel in teams

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:41.340
and help solve some of the world's more
complex problems.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.880
And since we are able to focus on them for a long period and don’t give up easily,

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.120
success is just a matter of time.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.780
THOSE OF YOU WHO HAVE SOME EXECUTIVE FUNCTION,

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.640
CAN NOW MAXIMIZE THEIR LEARNING FROM THIS VIDEO.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.860
JUST TURN OFF YOUTUBE, TAKE OUT PEN AND PAPER,

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:03.180
AND SUMMARIZE THE CONCEPT IN YOUR OWN SIMPLE LANGUAGE.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.880
IF YOU FAIL THIS TIME, TRY AGAIN AND THEN
DO IT.

